Both are made in the islets of Langerhans: insulin by the more numerous beta (β) cells, glucagon by alpha (α) cells.

Insulin (secreted when blood glucose rises) reduces blood glucose by:
1. increasing the rate of glucose uptake by body cells (e.g., skeletal muscles and fat cells);
2. promoting glycogenesis (glucose to glycogen) in liver;
3. increasing the use of glucose in cellular respiration;
4. promoting conversion of excess glucose to fats;
5. inhibiting gluconeogenesis (glucose synthesis).

Glucagon (secreted when blood glucose is below normal) raises blood glucose mainly by promoting hydrolysis of stored glycogen in liver; glucose is released into blood and taken up by cells for energy.

The adrenal medulla is the inner portion of the adrenal gland (one gland just above each kidney). It produces epinephrine (adrenaline) and norepinephrine (noradrenaline).

Fight or flight: when a person is stressed, the medulla secretes these hormones, which prepare the body by:
1. increased heart rate;
2. increased blood pressure;
3. dilation of the bronchioles;
4. elevation in blood glucose;
5. reduced blood flow to skin and digestive organs.

Control: secretion of the adrenal medulla is controlled by the nervous system (the cortex by anterior pituitary hormones).
Imbalance: over-secretion may cause hypertension and aggressive behaviour in routine life; under-secretion causes failure to combat emergency situations.
